I would support _the university_** doing so, after due process.  Would *you*
support the notion that businesses should be able to put comments in the
records of *employees* who xerox off 12 million copies of excerpts of Exodus
using company copiers and post them on bulletin boards all over the world?  

Keep in mind this is a sysadmin--he's getting paid to run the system, not
stuff the net with posts about armegeddon.  Students are (often) fee-paying
customers.  Still, they too should get comments if they are formally charged
with violating university rules about use of computer resources (following
due process, of course.)


]>He ought to be allowed to keep
]>his job upon promising never to do the like again, IMO.

]It was nice to see this...

I'm a nice guy.  The offender must have known what he was doing was "wrong,"
but a moral imperative to save our souls got the best of him.  If, after
due process, it is determined he abused his position (and that was implied
by the original post which asked what "punishment" would be appropriate),
then I think a comment in his record of employment is not out of order.
I think a discharge would be excessive, since the actual damage done can't
be accurately assessed, and is probably negligible.

 **The university should be the entitity putting things in students' records.
 As far as I'm concerned, sysadmins are overworked, underpaid button pushers
 who shouldn't have a microgram of authority over students.  Only formal boards
 of inquiry should ever be able to touch students records (with the exception
 of instructors entering grades).

