There are some regular posts on volume in news.lists.  The most
relevant is "NewsStats, article sizes".  You can use them to plan 
expiration or to find out little tidbits like "alt.binaries is 40%
of the volume in alt." :-)

Locally, I run a program which generates reports like this each week:

	From Sat Jan 22 23:54:03 EST 1994
	To   Sat Jan 29 23:50:42 EST 1994

	Totals for this period:
	320969 730240600 Totals

	By # of articles:
	11025 28578797   junk
	3021  1835326   control.cancel
	2466  4850028   alt.flame
	1837  3093183   talk.politics.guns
	1820  2436010   alt.music.alternative
	1610  2429685   soc.motss
	1556  2803431   rec.arts.tv.soaps
	1490  2485490   rec.sport.football.pro
	1489  2265413   alt.fan.rush-limbaugh
	1460  2688413   alt.atheism

	By Total volume:
	 846 40858632   alt.binaries.pictures.misc
	1152 40751659   alt.binaries.pictures.erotica
	11025 28578797   junk
	 430 15309407   alt.binaries.sounds.misc
	 524 13875999   alt.binaries.pictures.supermodels
	 318  9059673   alt.binaries.pictures.tasteless
	 213  8939510   alt.binaries.pictures.erotica.male
	 108  7172379   alt.binaries.multimedia
	 176  5742624   comp.mail.maps
	2466  4850028   alt.flame

and a similar report each day with daily statistics (first column is
article count, second is volume adjusted for crossposting) for the top
ten groups in each category.  "junk" represents every article we
receive for a group we don't carry; most of this volume is for alt
groups we've elected not to support.

Now I'll admit that 29 megs per week is chump change compared to what
we're processing, but at the same time it's more volume than all of
ba.*, k12.*, gnu.*, fr.*, biz.*, info.*, bionet.*, and vmsnet.*
put together, all of which we carry.  As another example, if we
dropped alt.binaries.* from our feed we could retain everything else
an extra day, making usenet that much more accessible for the
occasional user.  

And we're an unusually large site; we have over a gigabyte of disk space 
to run news on.  Even without alt.binaries, alt.* constitutes about
200 megabytes per week.  A site with only 200 or 400 megs of article
storage basically faces the choice of expiring groups at two or three 
days--ridiculously fast, since not everyone reads news that often--or 
start picking which groups/hierarchies to carry.
