Subject: obscenity - Waterloo focused on medium rather than message
Date: 1 Feb 1994 13:31:10 -0500
Message-ID: <2im75e$au2@ghost.McRCIM.McGill.EDU>

In a recent "uw.general" news article, a new mechanism
was described by which "obscene" Usenet newsgroups
may be reviewed following a formal complaint,
and possibly blocked from acceptance on campus.

It is certainly reassuring to know that faculty, students, and staff
all have a say on the UW Ethic Committee, and thus no decisions
will be taken precipitously.

What seems odd in the recent announcement is the UW administration's
focus on "the medium" rather than "the message".

If a "message" offends, it does so regardless of the medium.

One would hope that the UW Ethics committee would accept
complaints of obscenity, regardless of the medium.
Thus, obscene material in the library or student newspapers
would be fair game for formal complaints.

Can anyone confirm whether the Ethics committee has a broad mandate
to deal with obscenity in any form?

dj


