    I would like to remind readers at schools where certain Usenet groups
are banned that alternatives do exist. To read a banned group, one may:
    --Telnet or Gopher to a remote machine that receives the groups
      (there must be hundreds, or even thousands)
    --FTP to sites that offer GIFs or whatever one seeks
    --Recieve digestified forms of certain groups via email
 
    I urge you to explore these possibilities, and describe your findings
on the local Waterloo net for all to enjoy. To get the ball rolling, may
I suggest Gophering to the University of Wyoming, which maintains an
uncensored Usenet feed. Others exist, and they deserve mention at schools
which choose to decide for their students what types of expression are
not acceptable. In any case, such instruction points out to the censors
that their efforts are futile, and shows other users where they may go
to exercise their own freedom to choose.
 
  Don't let this happen! Fight censorhsip when it happens at your school
(as is the sad case here at UGA), and let everyone know that sources of
information are available which censors cannot touch without completely
severing Internet access.
 
Regards,
MSM
 

