The Kentucky Education Reform Act takes the 'local panel' approach,
but does include some oversight at the state level.  I support this
approach; some state control is necessary, and I can envision a need
for Federal oversight as well.

School reform is a quagmire.  It's obvious that those school systems 
which currently enjoy a high status will resist change; it's equally 
obvious that poorer schools are the impetus behind each of these 
initiatives.  Reform will be a tedious, lengthy process; I believe 
that either of these proposals will need far more than 5 years to 
generate measurable improvement.

Neither of these proposals address an issue I consider fundamental
to school reform; managing the educators themselves.  It is nearly
impossible to remove a poor teacher from most schools.  The teacher
who told our class that the Amazon River was in Africa (and threatened
to fail me if I ever corrected him in class again), the English teacher 
who routinely used phrases such as "it ain't never" and the teacher 
who issues Cs to students with 95 averages because "they aren't doing 
as well as they could" MUST be removed from the system before anything 
is really going to change.  (These are all actual cases; two of them 
were my instructors, and one was my brother's.)  We need merit pay,
competency evaluations, and a procedure for removing bad teachers;
once these are in place, we *may* be able to create some real reforms.

--Wes

