I'm a little confused by this.  It seems that you're expressing two,
contradictory, desires here.  On one hand, you agree that overregulation
is bad.  On the other, you call for more regulation ("a strong anti-censorship
policy").

Frankly, I'm a lot more worried about the loss of one individual's freedom
(to administer his site as he sees fit) than I am about so-called censorship at
electronic repositories.  

>>But as a strong opponent of any form of censorship, Noring believes removing
>>something for technical reasons is different than pulling something because
>>of concerns about content.

And I can probably come up with a compelling technical reason not to include
anything I don't want in an archive.  Or are you arguing for even more
rules and policies to help people determine if censorship is
technically or content-based?

>We cannot
>allow censorship of Constitutionally-protected materials to ever succeed, and
>DOUBLY so when the pressure is exerted from culturally/religiously/politically
>different lobbying groups *outside* the U.S.!

The Constitution is a body of work giving certain powers, and restricting
others, to the Federal Government of the United States.  The fourteenth
amendment extends some of these restrictions to the individual states.

The U.S. Consititution does not bind you, me, or Mike Regoli when not
acting on behalf of the state.  Nor does it bind New Zealand from
protesting the materials Mike makes available.

The Internet is not an absolute free-speech forum*, but it's very
open.  Policies made incumbent upon individuals and institutions
towards furthering the free-speech nature of the Internet will only
succeed in regularizing the Internet instead of the desired
effect of liberalizing it.

I find it interesting that people are calling for Mike to support
the "Constitutionally-protected" right of free speech, but at
the same time require him to make policy statements and to
abide by those policy statements.  Or does "free speech" not extend towards
being inconsistent and irrational?  "You can say or do anything you
want, as long as you abide by an a priori policy statement that's not
in-consistent [in our minds] and not irrational [in our minds]."

Welcome to the technocracy.  Frankly, I'm urging Mike to walk away
from the whole archive and be free.

*If you don't believe me, try posting an article to all the newsgroups
with the title "Global Alert: Jesus is coming soon" twice a day and see
how long your account or newsfeed lasts.)

