>In noring@netcom.com (Jon Noring) writes:

>Frankly, I'm a lot more worried about the loss of one individual's freedom
>(to administer his site as he sees fit) than I am about so-called censorship at
>electronic repositories.  

If this were Mike's site, he could do anything he pleases with it, and I'd
support his right to do that.  If it were his site, and he required that all
submitters must be male between 5'6" and 5'9" tall, with blond hair and blue
eyes with Aryan ancestry, I'd totally support his right to that policy -- I'd
vigorously defend it.  It is not, however, Mike's site, it's a public
institutional site, supported by state and federal dollars, as long as Mike
uses the equipment, and/or the name of CICA, or IU in describing it.  For it
to *truly* be Mike's site, he'd have to buy or rent his own computer and pay
for his own feed, etc.  It is clear that Mike removed Fanny Hill because he
feared that his archive could be closed down by those at CICA/IU who, he
perceived, didn't want to be bothered with complaints.  He even said so in one
of his early posts.  This fear is *proof* that CICA is NOT Mike's site in a
legal sense since someone else could pull the plug and he could only watch it
sink into electronic heaven.  Thus, the CICA archive is ultimately controlled
by a public institution, supported by state (Indiana) and probably federal
money, and thus comes under public scrutiny.  Mike unfortunately got caught
between the censorship forces led by the "New Zealand Censorship Society and
Cabal" and the anti-censorship forces.  I believe the anti-censorship forces
are on the higher moral ground in this instance.


>And I can probably come up with a compelling technical reason not to include
>anything I don't want in an archive.  Or are you arguing for even more rules
>and policies to help people determine if censorship is technically or
>content-based?

Yes, if they're necessary to fight censorship of a publicly-controlled archive
site.  And I'm not advocating "rules".  I hate "rules" myself.  A statement of
policy is different than "rules".  And a statement of policy like, for
example, the ALA is one of the most pro-libertarian policies I've ever seen.
(Politically I call myself a pragmatic libertarian, just so you know.)

Essentially, the statement would say that the CICA archive will not remove or
restrict any Windows-based software based on content, except where the content
is clearly not Constitutionally protected.  I'm sure Carl Kadie and the EFF
could come up with a better and more air-tight policy, but what I wrote gives
an idea of what I have in mind.  Then, when someone from the "New Zealand
Cabal" or the "Jesse Helms Fan Club" complains to Mike/CICA/IU that Grimm's
Fairy Tales is sexually suggestive and should be removed from CICA, Mike can
simply e-mail the policy back to them and state that IU fully backs this
policy.  That'll stop 99% of all the attempts at censorship quickly and
painlessly.  And the 1% better be willing to fight a battle since there'll be
a lot of people and organizations on the net, e.g. EFF, who'll make life
very miserable for the "Forces of Censorship".


>>We cannot allow censorship of Constitutionally-protected materials to ever
>>succeed, and DOUBLY so when the pressure is exerted from culturally/
>>religiously/politically different lobbying groups *outside* the U.S.!

>The Constitution is a body of work giving certain powers, and restricting
>others, to the Federal Government of the United States.  The fourteenth
>amendment extends some of these restrictions to the individual states.

Thanks for sharing the nuances of the U.S. Constitution.  Last I heard, the
Supreme Court ruled that Fanny Hill was "protected" material under the
Constitution -- that's good enough for me.  The Constitution does, by its
interpretation, "protect" certain materials from censorship by the State --
Free Speech and all that jazz.  CICA/IU is a State institution, subject to the
Constitution, the laws, and all court rulings.  I've even been told in private
e-mail with an attorney that I might have a strong case for a successful
lawsuit against IU for Mike's removal of "Fanny Hill".  I don't want to do
this (I'd rather work with IU to help them formulate a strong anti-censorship
policy for the CICA archive), so this is the last you'll hear about that from
me.


>The U.S. Consititution does not bind you, me, or Mike Regoli when not
>acting on behalf of the state.  Nor does it bind New Zealand from
>protesting the materials Mike makes available.

Mike's operation of CICA is done on behalf of the state.  Period.  CICA/IU
could be successfully sued, imho, for things Mike does with the archive that
harms another.  CICA/IU does have a tie to the CICA archive, whether you like
it or not.

And when I wrote my last post I was careful to differentiate between the right
of New Zealanders to express their views in the U.S., and allowing them to
actively succeed at censorship.  These are two totally different things.  Go
back and read my post if you didn't see the difference.  A public institution
in the U.S. should not allow any group from outside the U.S. to control
materials that are Constitutionally protected within the U.S.  That's my
philosophy -- I don't know if there's a legal basis for this belief, but to
me it sounds like "the way it ought to be", as long as the U.S. is a
politically separate entity from the rest of the world.


>The Internet is not an absolute free-speech forum*, but it's very open.
>Policies made incumbent upon individuals and institutions towards furthering
>the free-speech nature of the Internet will only succeed in regularizing the
>Internet instead of the desired effect of liberalizing it.

I'm an opponent of "political correctness" myself.  I'm not asking for 
regulation.  I'm asking for public sites, like the CICA archive, to implement
policies that are in line with the Constitution.  Public sites taking a stand
against allowing *anyone* to successfully remove protected materials is
necessary for the ultimate liberalizing of the Internet.  By not taking an
activist stand in this regard will only allow the "Forces of Censorship" to
continue to harass public sites like CICA into submission -- and I know this
is not what you want.  In libertarian heaven, the "Forces of Censorship" cannot
exist, but this is the Real World (tm), and they carry a lot of power and they
are anti-libertarian, and they'll go after the private sites as well.  On the
other hand, private sites should be protected from any requirements, except,
of course, when by their actions they prevent others from setting up their own
similar sites (sort of a monopoly, this is where my pragmatism comes in --
this is the proper role for government.)

Libertarian principles of a free Internet do require that public sites not
allow any special interest group to control what can and cannot be distributed.
Here's a case where properly written policy statements, though not exactly
libertarian by their very existence, best further the libertarian cause in the
Real World (tm), which is, what I gather from your post, important to you,
even if you don't call or identify yourself as a libertarian.


>I find it interesting that people are calling for Mike to support
>the "Constitutionally-protected" right of free speech, but at
>the same time require him to make policy statements and to
>abide by those policy statements.  Or does "free speech" not extend towards
>being inconsistent and irrational?  "You can say or do anything you
>want, as long as you abide by an a priori policy statement that's not
>in-consistent [in our minds] and not irrational [in our minds]."

Yes, as long as Mike represents a public site, which he does.  If he had his
own private site, my posts would sound exactly like yours.  You and I would
have no disagreement then.


>Welcome to the technocracy.  Frankly, I'm urging Mike to walk away
>from the whole archive and be free.

Mike can walk away from it if he likes, but it is not a private archive in the
truest sense.  Mike has done a great job in getting it started (his idea) and
keeping it going, but unfortunately, he is using state (and ultimately) federal
resources to do what he's doing -- CICA/IU is the real legal power behind the
CICA Windows archive, even if they let Mike do his thing, and they are
ultimately responsible in a legal sense.  And last I heard, CICA/IU is a
public entity.

