Thus said greg@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu (Gregory R. TRAVIS):
>You could argue that the FTP site is not like an office - that it's
>much more like a library (in the tired old analogy).  However, unlike
>a library, and much like a professor's office, access to FTP sites
>is commonly restricted to certain times of day and by number of
>concurrent users.  Furthermore, the content of the site is often much
>like a professor's office in that materials are very topic-specific.

Libraries are not commonly restricted to use at certain times of day?
Can I print out your message and use it to demand entry to any IU
library at 2am?

Libraries are not commonly restricted by topic?  Can I take your
message and bring it to the Jordan Hall Biology Library and demand
they carry more Chinese poetry?

Library stacks are not often restricted by number of concurrent users
because of underutilization; no IU library can hold an arbitrarily
large number of people without the Office of Risk Management taking a
rather strong interest.  Resources within a library, such as study
rooms, xerox machines, and UCS computing clusters do have decided
limitations on their use.

>>It is clear that Mike removed Fanny Hill because he
>>feared that his archive could be closed down by those at CICA/IU who, he
>>perceived, didn't want to be bothered with complaints.
>
>So, are you saying that being timid should be illegal?

Being timid is entirely legal.  In this case, of course, it seems to
have backfired to at least some extent.

The real point, IMHO, is that those who opposed the removal have won.
It's reasonably obvious that Mike removed the file without ever
considering what the consequences of its removal as far as public
outcry and potential publicity goes, and he acknowledges that at least
some aspects of the way it was handled (like claiming it was a result
of IU policy about maintaining adult materials) were mistaken.

Mike said he was trying to cover his ass.  Did it work?

Clearly nobody familiar with this incident will fail to at least
consider such questions if faced with a similar situation.

As far as I'm concerned, there probably isn't a constitutional
stipulation that Mike Regoli carry this material on that site, though
I'm open to being persuaded otherwise.  Mike may, IMHO, remove
material at whim or have a policy statement that says:

}  I'll put whatever I want up here and not put whatever I don't want
}  up here and if you don't like it get lost.

but, if that's the policy, it ought to be honestly stated.  I won't
claim it's a constitutional mandate or legal obligation; it's just
good manners.
