[Temporarily cross-posted to misc.legal.  There's some legal issues concerning
public institutions and censorship which I hope some of the legal eagles here
might be able to answer.]


In article greg@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu (Gregory R. TRAVIS) writes:
>In noring@netcom.com (Jon Noring) writes:

>>If this were Mike's site, he could do anything he pleases with it, and I'd
>>support his right to do that.

>How does your assesment that it's "not Mike's site" jive with the fact
>that he could simply walk away from it without fear of losing his
>job or be subject to possible criminal proceedings?

Simple.  CICA/IU is allowing him to use their facilities to run this archive,
but have not "officially" sanctioned the archive themselves.  Many
institutions of higher learning give a lot of freedom to their staff and
faculty to pursue research projects without "official" sanction, and to use
their facilities, within reason.  I know, I was a graduate student myself and
did all kinds of "unofficial" research.  But even when I did this "unofficial"
research, I was still bound by institutional rules, many of which were
established by Congress (environmental, etc.)  I'm sure if Mike had decided
instead to run an archive devoted to disseminating information about how
African-Americans are a danger to the Aryan race (this is just hypothetical,
folks, I'm sure he wouldn't ever do that), no doubt CICA/IU would have put the
kibosh on his little project as fast as a nuclear detonation, and I believe
Mike could not have legally stopped IU from shutting down his site since he
works for IU and is using their facilities and resources.

It is clear that CICA/IU is ultimately legally responsible for the archive.


>Through all this, it's not been made clear to me how the University's
>obligations vis-a-vis the Constitution translate into obligations on
>Mike.  That is, given that he's operating in a purely volunteer role.

He is a volunteer.  He created the archive.  Both of these facts are clear.
I won't argue with this.

But he also did it at the pleasure of IU, who could put the kibosh on his
project anytime they wish (Mike implied that himself), and he'd have no
recourse but to read 'em and weep.  If it were HIS list, then legally he could
take IU to court if they tried to take it away from him.  And if an attorney
believed I could successfully sue IU for what Mike did (of course, not proof
in and of itself), then I at least have come to the conclusion that CICA/IU
is legally responsible for the Windows archive, whether they specifically
sanctioned it or not.

(Note, as I said in a prior post, the CICA archive has become so well-known
that it is being distributed via CD-ROM to those without net access.  Thus,
I saw a potential loss of income from my Windows application Shareware book
because of the decision to remove it based *solely* on speech content.  Yet,
"Fanny Hill" was declared to be "not obscene" by the Supreme Court in 1966,
so it is a "protected work".  You can find "Fanny Hill", the paper version at
most public libraries, who will loan it out to anybody (minors included).
Seeing that the CICA archive is supported by a public, not private,
institution, this act of censorship of a protected work solely based on its
speech content by an IU agent is possibly depriving me of income as well as
depriving many people who receive the CD-ROM who would be interested in it.)


>Are you willing to say that an English professor, who teaches
>English literature and is known to loan his or her books to students,
>is bound by the Constitution to carry a copy of "Fanny Hill" if
>someone requests it?

No, of course not.  But if the professor sets up a public archive site whose
purpose is to receive and disseminate *to anybody* Windows applications, then
denying a Windows application solely on speech content (without a pre-written
policy statement approved by the legal people at IU), is probably illegal
since, again, IU is legally responsible for the archive and they are a state-
run (public) institution, bound by a myriad of laws and Constitutional rulings.


>What if that same English professor starts a windows FTP site, using
>the PC that his department donated?  How about if a student
>calls up and wishes to know about Fanny Hill?  Is the professor, a civil
>servant at work during his or her office hours, bound by law to discuss
>the book he or she has, that's sitting on a publicly-provided
>bookshelf, in a publicly-provided office, over a publicly-provided phone?

This is getting a little silly.  You're comparing apples and oranges.


>>It is clear that Mike removed Fanny Hill because he
>>feared that his archive could be closed down by those at CICA/IU who, he
>>perceived, didn't want to be bothered with complaints.

>So, are you saying that being timid should be illegal?

No, but life is not a bowl of cherries, either.  Actually, Mike is not the
one who is ultimately responsible, it is IU, since Mike is a defacto
representative of IU.  If anything, it is IU that did the illegal act of
censoring "Fanny Hill".  Mike just did what he felt best at the moment (as I
said, I could have made the same decision -- hindsight is much clearer than
foresight.)  I could not successfully sue Mike (that's clear), but I could
successfully sue IU since they did not establish a clear and legal policy at
the time when I uploaded "Fanny Hill".  All Mike would do would testify in
court during the hearing of the lawsuit (don't worry, IU, I do not intend to
file a lawsuit -- I'd rather help you draft a progressive anti-censorship
policy so that this doesn't become an issue again anywhere.)


>Now, you can argue that Mike shouldn't have been timid, that
>self-interested self-censorship is not a valid excuse for what
>he did - I might agree with you.  However, to force him to do as YOU
>want, in the name of the First Amendment seems to be terribly
>disingenuous.

If this were HIS site (in a legal sense), then you have a correct argument.
But the CICA archive is not HIS, it belongs to IU, and they are legally
responsible for what goes on in it.  We should be arguing whether IU can
legally remove Fanny Hill from the archive site based solely on speech content
and the circumstances at the time it was uploaded to the archive.

As I said, Mike just got caught between two large forces.  His best course of
action is to setup a policy that meets current legal requirements for a public
University.  I encourage him to emulate the policies that most public
libraries use, which I believe is called the ALA policy.  I'm sure the EFF
would be more than happy to help him write it, and I will help anyway I can
as well.


>I appreciate your attempts to control his
>behavior based upon the fact that he's using state-owned equipment. 
>However, I think that enough examples can be found in which individuals,
>employed by the state and using state-owned equipment, are not
>subject to constitutional restraint that it'll be difficult to bind
>Mike to the same restraints.

I believe that ultimately it can be shown that the CICA Windows archive is
the legal responsibility of IU, whether IU wants that responsibility or not.
Since IU is a public institution, they are bound by the laws and court rulings
that apply to public institutions of their type.  My understanding is that
the removal of Fanny Hill, in this situation, borders on violation of law.
Could someone out there in misc.legal-land clarify this?


>Here's a question for Carl Kadie, or perhaps counselor Goodwin:  What's
>the legal rational for binding state institutions, such as a university,
>to constitutional restraints?  I can imagine a state, or the university
>itself, adopting a "no censorship" law, but what's the Constitution
>got to do with it?  I realize it must have something to
>do with the Fourteenth Amendment and/or the receipt of federal
>funds but...

Good question.


>All that says is that you, me, or Mike cannot be punished for publishing
>Fanny Hill as private individuals.  It says NOTHING about you, me, or Mike
>being OBLIGATED to publish it as private OR public individuals.

In this situation, Mike set up an incoming directory by which he solicited all
Windows applications for inclusion into the archive.  Every so often, Mike
looked at the stuff in this directory, and determined whether it should be
included in the archive.  It is clear that he can legally reject stuff which
is not a Windows application, which is commercial and/or copyrighted, which is
clearly illegal, which is garbled because it wasn't uploaded correctly, which
did not have an accompanying *.txt file describing what it is (one of the few
requirements he established), and probably a few other more technically-based
criteria.  He did not, as far as I could tell, exactly spell out by speech
content what was and was not acceptable.  And even if he did, it is the speech
content stuff that begins to fall under Constitutional/legal aspects, since the
archive is ultimately the legal responsibility of a public institution.

Mike cannot be punished by us for what he did, and it behooves IU to not
punish him either should it be established that what he did was illegal, since
he was acting in good faith as an agent of IU, even if he did not perceive of
himself at the time of acting in the capacity as an agent of IU.

Interesting discussion.  Hope the legal-eagles can clarify some of these
issues.

Jon Noring

