when i first heard of this, my first reaction was utter disbelief.  it
was as if the events of a few years ago, the alt.* banning, had never
happened.  i was astounded and outraged that the administration had the
gall to simply repeat their blundering heavy-handed censorship as if
the advisory committee on network news didn't exist and the user
community didn't matter.  finally, the administration presumably
thought, this boil on the backside of humanity known as usenet would
cease to cause trouble and the administration could pat itself on the
back for decisive action and a progressive, politically-correct job
well done.

however, there are many people who've posted cynical or outraged
comments far more eloquently than i, so i shall refrain from further
inflamed rhetoric.

i think what is interesting is that usenet at waterloo has been under
almost constant attack since i started here, in '88 (yes, i remember
the r.h.f mess).

why, exactly, is it that this mode of communication has been attacked?
it is often lauded for its chaotic free-for-all which represents,
perhaps, the ultimate expression of freedom of speech.  why is that so
threatening to people?  perhaps it is exactly that.  the fact that no
voices are heard louder than others.  and that some voices, used to
being heard louder, suddenly find themselves deprived of this power.

i wonder, have other media been subjected to this kind of assault?  i
know that under oppressive regimes, censorship is often seen (by the
controlling party) as necessary for the preservation of order.  it
often has led to disastrous consequences.  but in democratic societies,
have new forms of communication always stirred up such controversy?

when the printing press was invented, were there people clamoring for
its destruction on the basis that something offensive had or would be
made with it?  how about radio?  the telephone?  fax machines?  i
honestly would like to know.

usenet truly is a medium like no other.  phones and fax machines are
one-to-one, radio, television and newspapers are one-to-many, with (by
necessity) someone determining what gets reported and what doesn't.
but usenet is many-to-many, with *no* person in power determining what
you can or cannot read, whose opinions you may or may not listen to.

except at the university of waterloo.

