If he'd posted 1000 articles of the same length about the weather in his
part of the US _I_ would be just as annoyed, given that I'm in the UK.
I doubt whether there is _anything_ that should be sprayed across all
newsgroups that the poster can get his scripts on. If you define posting
to 1000 groups as an `accident', I'd hate to see what your insurance
premiums are like. :-)

> "make.money.fast" and "Jesus is cumming soon" both offend people's
> delicate sensitivities.  People don't behave on Usenet like they do in
> real life.  Here, people will say/do things they would never consider
> face to face.  And here everybody can be a petty dictator (and they
> do.)

And typing `f' is a lot easier than making a phone call or writing a
letter. Maybe the newsreaders should count to 10 for you. 0.1 * :-)

> In real life, posting "make.money.fast" to 1000 newsgroups would be
> called "Telemarketing".  Nobody likes it, but nobody gets upset about
> it either (unless maybe they call you at 8am on a saturday morning
> ...)  And they certainly don't respond to it by calling up 1000 more
> people telling them that 'person X' needs to lose his job and have his
> fingers chopped off.

Actually, it bugs the hell out of me whatever the time and I usually put
the phone down immediately. If I'm feeling bad tempered, I ask them to hold
for a while and ignore the phone until I hear the dial tone (usually takes
5-10 minutes :-). In that case though, it's the telemarketers who pay.
When _you_ are paying to receive the junk mail/fax/article, the irritation
is far worse. It's the relative difficulty and cost of phoning/faxing
1000 people that (usually) prevents flames in other media. I do know people
who respond to junk faxes by `fax bombing' the originators - they just tape
the junk fax into a loop and feed it back zillions of times until the far
end is likely to have run out of paper. Again, this is not logical as it
costs them far more than the original junk fax, but it makes them happier.

In this particular case the perceived offence was compounded by the fact
that the guy was some sort of sysadmin, who should have known better, and
the fact that his punishment appears to have been a week's paid leave
hasn't really helped. (If _I_ could get a week's paid leave by roboposting
you'd be seeing "Make Money Fast" articles from me regularly. :-)
