I wonder whether the sort of information now banned
at the University of Waterloo in electronic form will soon be physically
removed from the UW Libraries, or for that matter, how a campus newspaper,
the Gazette, available both in print and electronic media,
might be affected.

I presume it is common knowledge that information that, at first blush,
might seem controversial is readily available in the UW Library:
e.g. _Playboy_ is available on microfilm;
a book denying the holocaust, _The Hoax of the Twentieth Century_
is another random example.

Although the UW Ethics committee has not yet deemed it necessary to censor,
for example, alt.binaries.pictures.erotica, this is a good one to
consider.

Imagine yourself in the library microfilm room, with a large display
lit up in front of you, as you scan through an issue of Playboy.
Apart from the technology, this is otherwise identical to
someone viewing an image in a computer lab.

Will one be banned, and not the other?   If nothing else,
the UW administration should ensure its policy is consistent.

dj


