MOUKDAD,HAIDAR ALI,MR <FPBS000@MUSICB.MCGILL.CA> wrote:
>
>What kinda idea is that. You want to say that if somone said "you
>should be killed", he/she is expressing an idea? You don't see the
>the difference? Advocating crime is a crime not an innocent expression
>of an idea.

Perhaps that's true in Canada, but not in the U.S.  

"[C]onstitutional guarantees of free speech and free press do not permit a 
State to forbid or proscribe advocacy of the use of force or of law violation
except where such advocacy is directed to inciting or producing imminent
lawless action and is likely to incite or produce such action.  As we said 
in Noto v. United States, 367 U.S. 290, 297-98 (1961), 'the mere abstract
teaching . . . of the moral propriety of even moral necessity for a resort to
force and violence, is not the same as preparing a group for violent action
and steeling it to such action.'"

					Brandenburg v. Ohio, 395 U.S. 444
					(1969)

Now, if someone just walked up to you in isolation and said "you should be
killed (or raped, or whatever),"  that's not going to produce 
a danger of "imminent
lawless action."  However, if he said such a thing in the presence of an
angry mob, that would probably be different under our First Amendment.


					Ken  kbullock@acpub.duke.edu


